INPUT DOCUMENTATION v1 (identical across languages). Inputs are JSON objects. Integers below are JSON integers, not strings, floats or booleans. Arrays preserve order. The harness converts this wire representation to the run signature; this is not language syntax.
Fields: budget: integer in [0,512]; jobs: array of at most24 objects, each with exactly id,cost,deps. id: integer, all job IDs together are exactly0..n-1 in any array order. cost: integer in [0,64]. deps: array of at most24 valid job IDs; repeats, self-references and cycles are allowed. deps are IDs, not array positions.

Implement run(jobs,budget)->Summary(order,spent,blocked). Job fields id,cost,deps. Jobs have unique IDs exactly0..n-1, arrive in arbitrary order,0<=n<=24,0<=cost<=64,0<=budget<=512. deps contains valid IDs, may repeat and may contain self-dependencies or cycles. Start with no completed jobs. Repeatedly choose the LOWEST ID among unfinished jobs whose every dependency is completed and whose cost<=remaining budget; execute it, append its ID to order, subtract its cost. A zero-cost job can run at zero budget. If no eligible fitting job remains, stop. spent is total executed cost. blocked counts EVERY unfinished job, whether blocked by dependencies or budget. Do not stop merely because a lower-ID job is unaffordable.
Task family: scheduler. Source-only comprehension. Return one JSON object: predictions (SIX complete normalized Summary objects for the inputs below); witness (any valid input yielding {"order": [2, 0], "spent": 3, "blocked": 1}; unspecified output fields can vary); direct_calls (application functions directly called by run, ignoring constructors/builtins/standard-library methods and util.reverse); impacted (functions in logic whose output can change for identical direct arguments if affordable changes cost<=remaining to cost+3<=remaining; include the edited predicate itself); fault_location (the predicate responsible for that policy); counterexample (any valid input distinguishing original from that mutant). Use plain function names. Output byte values as lowercase hex. Optional top-level notes is a string. No tools. Return JSON only.
